  <title>8.5.2: Interpreting Graphs to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02160</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.5.2: Interpreting Graphs to Solve Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>166ff21d-5944-4663-b361-e7f96e013b2e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity </title>
      <note>
        <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
      
      <para>Julio is solving three equations by graphing.</para>
      <para>\((x−5)(x−3)=0\)</para>
      <para>\((x−5)(x−3)=-1\)</para>
      <para>\((x−5)(x−3)=-4\)</para>
      <para>To solve the first equation, \((x−5)(x−3)=0\), he graphed \(y=(x−5)(x−3)\) and then looked for the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain why the \(x\)-intercepts can be used to solve \((x−5)(x−3)=0\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The solutions to \((x−5)(x−3)=0\) are \(x\)-values that make the expression have a value of 0. At the \(x\)-intercepts, the \(y\)-value of the expression \((x−5)(x−3)\) is 0, so the \(x\)-intercepts give the solutions.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Graph \(y=(x−5)(x−3)\). What are the solutions?</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="ALT TEXT: THE PARABOLA OPENS UP AND HAS A VERTEX AT THE POINT (4, NEGATIVE 1). THE \(x\)-intercepts ARE (3, 0) AND (5, 0).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_03_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>The solutions are \(x=5\) and \(x=3\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para>To solve the second equation, Julio rewrote it as \((x−5)(x−3)+1=0\). He then graphed \(y=(x−5)(x−3)+1\).</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Graph \(y=(x−5)(x−3)+1\). Then, use the graph to solve the equation. Be prepared to explain how you used the graph for solving.
 </item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="THE PARABOLA OPENS UP AND HAS A VERTEX AT THE POINT (4, 0). THE \(x\)-intercepts IS (4, 0).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_03_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>The solution is 4 for \((x−5)(x−3)+1=0\). The solution is the \(x\)-coordinate of the \(x\)-intercept.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para>Solve the third equation using Julio's strategy of graphing the equations.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>What are the solutions to \(y=(x-5)(x-3)+4\)?</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="THE PARABOLA OPENS UP AND HAS A VERTEX AT THE POINT (4, 3). THE \(x\)-intercepts IS (4, 3).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_03_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>There are no solutions for \((x−5)(x−3)+4=0\). The graph has no \(x\)-intercept.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Think about the graphing strategy used to solve the equations and the solutions you found. View the Desmos graphs of
  all three equations.
</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="View of Desmos Graph of all three equations">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_03_04.png"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
</solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Why might it be helpful to rearrange each equation to equal 0 on one side and then graph the expression on the non-zero side?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>When a quadratic expression is equal to 0 and we want to find the unknown values, we can think of it as finding the zeros of a quadratic function, or finding the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of that function.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>How many solutions does each of the three equations have?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter the number of solutions for each equation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>There are two solutions for the first equation, one solution for the second equation, and no solution for the last equation.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            
            
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
              <item>Graph \(y=(x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=-1\) on the same coordinate plane.</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>How do the equations \(y=(x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=-1\) help solve the equation \((x−3)(x−5)=-1\)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The solution to \((x−3)(x−5)=-1\) is the \(x\)-value of the intersection point of the two graphs of \(y=(x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=-1\). This solution is 4.</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH A VERTEX AT (4, NEGATIVE 1) AND \(x\)-intercepts OF 3 AND 5. A HORIZONTAL DOTTED LINE IS GRAPHED ATY EQUALS NEGATIVE 1.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_03_05.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <div class="os-raise-ib-content" data-schema-version="1.0" class="RFM1">
        <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
          <item>Graph \(y=(x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=0\) on the same coordinate plane.</item>
        </list>
      
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>How do the equations \(y=(x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=0\) help solve the equation \((x−3)(x−5)=0\)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The solutions to \((x−3)(x−5)=0\) are the \(x\)-values of the intersection points of the two graphs of \(y=(x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=0\). These solutions are 3 and 5.</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH A VERTEX AT (4, NEGATIVE 1) AND \(x\)-intercepts OF 3 AND 5. A HORIZONTAL DOTTED LINE IS GRAPHED AT Y EQUALS 0.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_03_06.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <div class="os-raise-ib-content" data-schema-version="1.0" class="RFM1">
        <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
          <item>Graph \(y = (x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=3\) on the same coordinate plane.</item>
        </list>
      
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>How do the equations \(y = (x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=3\) help solve the equation \((x−3)(x−5)=3\)?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The solutions to \((x−3)(x−5)=3\) are the \(x\)-values of the intersection points of the two graphs of \(y=(x−3)(x−5)\) and \(y=3\). These solutions are 2 and 6.</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH A VERTEX AT (4, NEGATIVE 1) AND \(x\)-intercepts OF 3 AND 5. A HORIZONTAL DOTTED LINE IS GRAPHED ATY EQUALS 3.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_03_07.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Use the graphs you created in questions 1 – 3 to help you find a few other equations of the form \((x−3)(x−5)=z\) that have whole-number solutions.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>There are many different answers. For example:</para>
          <para>\((x−3)(x−5)=8\), \((x−3)(x−5)=15\), \((x−3)(x−5)=24\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Some of the values of \(z\) that you might have found are –1, 0, 3, 8, 15, 24, etc. Find a pattern in the values of \(z\) that give whole-number solutions.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Starting at \(x=-1\), add 1, then 3, then 5, and so on, to find the next \(x\)-value that yields whole-number solutions.</para>
          <para>Because the expressions \(x−3\) and \(x−5\) represent numbers whose difference is 2, the values of \(z\) have the form \(-1 \cdot 1\), \(0 \cdot 2\), \(1 \cdot 3\), \(2 \cdot 4\), and so on.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Without solving, determine if \((x−5)(x−3)=120\) and \((x−5)(x−3)=399\) have whole-number solutions. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Both have whole-number solutions. The values of \(x\) that produce whole-number solutions are all 1 less than a square number.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>

    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Use graphing technology to determine the number of solutions for the equation \((x+3)(x−6)=−22\). Then find any solutions.</para>
    </note>

    <section>
      <title>Interpreting Graphs to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
      <para>Let's analyze and interpret the graphs of different quadratic equations.</para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para>First, let's look at the equation \((x−4)(x−7)=0\).</para>
      <para>We will graph the equation \(y=(x−4)(x−7)\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP AND PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (4, 0) AND (7, 0).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>This graph has two \(x\)-intercepts.</para>
      <para>The solutions to \((x−4)(x−7)=0\) are \(x\)-values that make the expression have a value of 0.</para>
      <para>At the \(x\)-intercepts, the \(y\)-value of the expression \((x−4)(x−7)\) is 0, so the \(x\)-intercepts give the solutions.</para>
      <para>The \(x\)-intercepts and solutions to the equation are 4 and 7.</para>
      <para>Note that the values of 4 and 7 could be referred to as <term class="glossary-tooltip">solutions</term> of the equation, <term class="glossary-tooltip">\(x\)-intercepts</term> of the graph, <term class="glossary-tooltip">roots</term> of the equation, or <term class="glossary-tooltip">zeros</term> of the equation.</para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para>Let's look at a different example.</para>
      <para>\((x−4)(x−7)=−2.25\)</para>
      <para>First, we rewrite the equation to set it equal to 0.</para>
      <para>\((x−4)(x−7)+2.25=0\)</para>
      <para>We will graph the equation \(y=(x−4)(x−7)+2.25\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH A VERTEX AT (5.5, 0).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_05_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>This graph has one \(x\)-intercept. It is an example of a double root at 5.5. A <term  class="glossary-tooltip">double root</term> is a root that appears twice in the solution of an algebraic equation.</para>
      <para>The solution is the \(x\)-coordinate of the \(x\)-intercept.</para>
      <para>So, the solution to \((x−4)(x−7)=−2.25\) is 5.5.</para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Example 3</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para>Let's look at one last example.</para>
      <para>\((x−4)(x−7)=-5\)</para>
      <para>First, we rewrite the equation to set it equal to 0.</para>
      <para>\((x−4)(x−7)+5=0\)</para>
      <para>We will graph the equation \(y=(x−4)(x−7)+5\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP. THE GRAPH DOES NOT TOUCH OR CROSS THE X-AXIS. THE GRAPH PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (4, 5) AND (7, 5).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_05_03.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>The graph has no \(x\)-intercepts.</para>
      <para>So, there are no real solutions for \((x−4)(x−7)+5=0\).</para>
      <para>Remember, when a <term  class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic expression</term> is equal to 0 and we want to find the unknown values, we can think of it as finding the zeros of a <term  class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic function</term>, or finding the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of that <term  class="glossary-tooltip">function</term>.</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Interpreting Graphs to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
      <para>Determine the number of solutions for the equation \((x−4)(x−9)=−6.25\). Then find any solutions.</para>
      <para>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course.</para>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Here is how to analyze the graph of the equation to find any solutions:</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Rewrite the equation to set it equal to 0.</para>
            <para> \((x−4)(x−9)+6.25=0\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Graph the equation \(y=(x−4)(x−9)+6.25\).</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH A VERTEX AT THE POINT (6.5, 0).">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_05_04.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Identify the \(x\)-intercepts from the graph.</para>
            <para> There is one \(x\)-intercept at \((6.5, 0)\)</para>
            <para> This means there is one solution.</para>
            <para> The solution is 6.5.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
